var tipuesearch = {"pages":[{"title":" fpm-watch ","text":"fpm-watch fpm-watch is an fpm plugin that reruns fpm build , test , or run when relevant files change. It uses fpm's dependency\ngraph, with optional dependency watching, OS sleep, and a restart supervisor. CI Status OS Compiler Version fpm ubuntu 24.04 gfortran 16.1.0 0.13.0 ✅ ubuntu 24.04 ifx 2026.1.1 0.13.0 ✅ macos 26 gfortran 16.1.0 0.13.0 ✅ windows 2025 gfortran 16.1.0 0.13.0 ✅ Installation Use fpm 0.13.0 (the version used in CI) and a Fortran compiler: git clone https://github.com/gha3mi/fpm-watch.git cd fpm-watch\nfpm install --profile release Ensure fpm and the installed fpm-watch executable are on PATH . Usage Run from your project's directory: fpm-watch [watch options] <build|test|run> [fpm arguments...] fpm-watch build\nfpm-watch test fpm-watch run\nfpm-watch --watch-low-cpu --watch-deps run --example my_example Place watcher options before the subcommand; subsequent arguments go to fpm.\nTarget lists such as run --target first,second follow fpm's selection.\nOn source changes, run / test select affected targets unless explicitly chosen. By default, the command runs at startup and after changes. Changes are checked\nbetween command runs. The watch list includes the project manifest, relevant\nsources, resolved Fortran includes, and quoted C headers. Manifest edits refresh settings and the watch list, then run the command. Failed\nrefreshes retain the previous settings and list until a later edit or rescan\nsucceeds. Use --watch-rescan 2 to discover added sources periodically. Stop with Ctrl+C , which also interrupts an active command, or create .fpm-watch.stop in the project directory (checked between commands). Remove\nthat file before restarting. User interrupts do not trigger supervisor restarts. Watch options Value options accept --watch-option VALUE or --watch-option=VALUE .\nFlags marked [=false] enable a setting; append =false to disable it. Times are in seconds;\ndefaults appear in the configuration block. Option Effect --help , -h , --watch-help Show help and exit. --watch-deps[=false] , --watch-no-deps Enable/disable dependency sources and manifests, including feature/profile dependencies. --watch-low-cpu[=false] , --watch-no-low-cpu Use OS sleep/busy waiting. --watch-poll SEC Poll interval, clamped to at least 0.05. --watch-debounce SEC Wait after a change before checking again and running. --watch-rescan SEC , --watch-no-rescan Periodically refresh the watch list and run if it changes; 0 disables rescans. --watch-run-on-start , --watch-no-run-on-start Run immediately/wait for a change. --watch-once[=false] Initialize, run if run-on-start is enabled, and exit with fpm's status (0 without a run). --watch-ignore GLOB , --watch-include GLOB Exclude/restrict paths; repeatable. Quote globs against shell expansion. --watch-feature NAME Enable a watcher plugin; repeatable (currently stats , an internal counter). --watch-fpm PATH Select the fpm executable by path or name. --watch-silent-fpm[=false] Suppress both fpm output streams. --watch-log-format FORMAT Select text , json , or logfmt . --watch-quiet , -q Watcher errors only, except enabled output protocol events. --watch-verbose[=N] , -v Raise verbosity to N (default 1, maximum 2); adds configuration and timings. --watch-very-verbose , -vv Verbosity 2; lists files after refreshes. --watch-print-files[=false] List up to 200 startup paths and raise verbosity to 2. --watch-debug[=false] Enable additional debug records. --watch-output-markers[=false] Frame command stderr in structured mode; see below. --watch-auto-restart[=false] Restart on nonzero watcher exit; command failures during continuous watching keep the watcher running. --watch-restart-delay SEC Wait before restarting. --watch-restart-max N Restart limit after initial launch; 0 means unlimited. --watch-self PATH Explicit watcher executable path for the supervisor. Ignore rules override include rules; watched manifests bypass both.\nWatcher plugins differ from fpm build features ( --features after the subcommand). Configuration CLI flags override fpm.toml settings, except include/ignore/feature flags append\nto lists and --watch-verbose only raises verbosity. Built-in defaults: [extra.fpm-watch] poll = 0.5 debounce = 0.2 rescan = 0.0 run-on-start = true once = false deps = false low-cpu = false silent-fpm = false print-files = false debug = false verbosity = 0 log-format = \"text\" fpm-executable = \"fpm\" output-markers = false auto-restart = false restart-delay = 1.0 restart-max = 0 self = \"\" ignore = [] include = [] features = [] Logging and integrations Text logs are timestamped without ANSI colors; watcher errors go to stderr.\nQuiet mode controls watcher logs; --watch-silent-fpm controls fpm output. For JSON Lines or logfmt, all watcher records, including errors, go to stdout ;\nraw fpm/model output and compiler/runtime diagnostics go to stderr. Keep the\nstreams separate when parsing records: fpm-watch --watch-log-format json build > watch.jsonl 2 > fpm.log\nfpm-watch --watch-log-format logfmt test > watch.log 2 > fpm.log Each record contains timestamp (RFC 3339 with milliseconds and UTC offset), level , event , and message (JSON) or msg (logfmt), and is flushed immediately.\nJSON attributes retain their types; logfmt arrays use quoted JSON. For automation, use events and attributes: command.started / command.finished share cycle_id ; completion includes exit_code , duration_ms , and interrupted . watch.changed includes paths . At verbosity 1, watch.configuration reports\neffective settings at startup and after successful refreshes. The log format stays fixed across manifest refreshes and supervisor restarts;\nchanging it requires restarting the watcher. For per-command output attribution, enable --watch-output-markers with a\nstructured format: fpm-watch --watch-log-format = json --watch-output-markers --watch-silent-fpm = false --watch-fpm /path/to/fpm build Stderr brackets raw output with @@fpm-watch:started:ID@@ and @@fpm-watch:finished:ID@@ . ID matches the opaque cycle_id (e.g. 0:1 ), unique\nacross supervisor restarts. Streams can arrive in either order. With markers,\nboth command events remain visible in quiet mode. Consume markers as protocol\nrecords. Development fpm build --profile debug --verbose\nfpm build --profile release --verbose\nfpm test fpm install --prefix .\n./bin/fpm-watch --watch-low-cpu --watch-once test python test/integration.py --watcher ./bin/fpm-watch\npython test/platform_regression.py ./bin/fpm-watch\nfortitude check\nford README.md Use ./bin/fpm-watch.exe on Windows. Python checks use only the standard library\nand temporary projects. Fortitude and FORD are optional local tools; FORD writes\ndocs to doc/ . CI uses ci-release (GNU -O1 ) on macOS, release elsewhere,\nand the debug profile for tests. Bash completion mkdir -p ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions\ncp completion.bash ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/fpm-watch source ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/fpm-watch Developer Info Seyed Ali Ghasemi","tags":"home","url":"index.html"},{"title":"watch_feature_t – fpm-watch ","text":"type, public :: watch_feature_t Base feature type (override callbacks to implement behavior). Inherited by type~~watch_feature_t~~InheritedByGraph type~watch_feature_t watch_feature_t type~feature_box_t feature_box_t type~feature_box_t->type~watch_feature_t p type~stats_feature_t stats_feature_t type~stats_feature_t->type~watch_feature_t type~feature_manager_t feature_manager_t type~feature_manager_t->type~feature_box_t feat type~watcher_t watcher_t type~watcher_t->type~feature_manager_t fm Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Type Type This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a derived type to the parent type which it\nextends. Dashed arrows point from a derived type to the other\ntypes it contains as a components, with a label listing the name(s) of\nsaid com