f90 sourcefile~watch_engine.f90->sourcefile~watch_log.f90 sourcefile~watch_engine.f90->sourcefile~watch_cli.f90 sourcefile~watch_feature_factory.f90 watch_feature_factory.f90 sourcefile~watch_engine.f90->sourcefile~watch_feature_factory.f90 sourcefile~watch_engine.f90->sourcefile~watch_platform.f90 sourcefile~watch_feat_stats.f90 watch_feat_stats.f90 sourcefile~watch_feat_stats.f90->sourcefile~watch_log.f90 sourcefile~watch_feature_factory.f90->sourcefile~watch_log.f90 sourcefile~watch_feature_factory.f90->sourcefile~watch_feat_stats.f90 sourcefile~watch_platform.f90->sourcefile~watch_log.f90 Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Source File Source File This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a file to a file which it depends on. A file\nis dependent upon another if the latter must be compiled before the former\ncan be. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code !> Miscellaneous string utilities. !! !! Provides small helpers for formatting and manipulating allocatable strings !! and string arrays. module watch_util use fpm_strings , only : string_t implicit none private public ftoa , trim_or_default , trim_or_empty , join_csv , starts_with contains !> Compare a case-sensitive, right-trimmed prefix; an empty prefix matches every string. pure logical function starts_with ( s , prefix ) result ( ok ) character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: s , prefix integer :: n n = len_trim ( prefix ) if ( n <= 0 ) then ok = . true . else if ( len_trim ( s ) < n ) then ok = . false . else ok = ( s ( 1 : n ) == prefix ( 1 : n )) end if end function starts_with !> Format a real using two decimal places by default and a leading zero. !! !! Optional `precision` is clamped to 0..16. Fixed output that overflows the !! 64-character buffer falls back to scientific notation. Nonfinite values !! are not rejected here; typed real logging handles them separately. pure function ftoa ( x , precision ) result ( s ) real , intent ( in ) :: x integer , intent ( in ), optional :: precision character ( len = :), allocatable :: s character ( len = 64 ) :: buf character ( len = 16 ) :: format integer :: digits digits = 2 if ( present ( precision )) digits = max ( 0 , min ( precision , 16 )) write ( format , \"(a,i0,a)\" ) \"(f0.\" , digits , \")\" write ( buf , format ) x if ( index ( buf , \"*\" ) > 0 ) write ( buf , \"(es24.16e3)\" ) x s = trim ( adjustl ( buf )) if ( s ( 1 : 1 ) == \".\" ) s = \"0\" // s if ( s ( 1 : 2 ) == \"-.\" ) s = \"-0\" // s ( 2 :) end function ftoa !> Trim an allocatable string or return a default if empty/unallocated. pure function trim_or_default ( s , default ) result ( r ) character ( len = :), allocatable , intent ( in ) :: s character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: default character ( len = :), allocatable :: r if ( allocated ( s )) then if ( len_trim ( s ) > 0 ) then r = trim ( s ) else r = default end if else r = default end if end function trim_or_default !> Trim an allocatable string or return an empty string if unallocated. pure function trim_or_empty ( s ) result ( r ) character ( len = :), allocatable , intent ( in ) :: s character ( len = :), allocatable :: r if ( allocated ( s )) then r = trim ( s ) else r = \"\" end if end function trim_or_empty !> Join a list of strings into a comma-separated value string. !! !! Empty entries are skipped. If all entries are empty (or the array is not !! allocated), returns the provided `empty` placeholder string. pure function join_csv ( a , empty ) result ( s ) type ( string_t ), allocatable , intent ( in ) :: a (:) character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: empty character ( len = :), allocatable :: s character ( len = :), allocatable :: tmp integer :: i tmp = \"\" if ( allocated ( a )) then do i = 1 , size ( a ) if ( len_trim ( a ( i )% s ) == 0 ) cycle if ( len ( tmp ) > 0 ) tmp = tmp // \",\" tmp = tmp // trim ( a ( i )% s ) end do end if if ( len ( tmp ) == 0 ) then s = empty else s = tmp end if end function join_csv end module watch_util","tags":"","url":"sourcefile/watch_util.f90.html"},{"title":"watch_feat_stats.f90 – fpm-watch","text":"This file depends on sourcefile~~watch_feat_stats.f90~~EfferentGraph sourcefile~watch_feat_stats.f90 watch_feat_stats.f90 sourcefile~watch_feature_api.f90 watch_feature_api.f90 sourcefile~watch_feat_stats.f90->sourcefile~watch_feature_api.f90 sourcefile~watch_log.f90 watch_log.f90 sourcefile~watch_feat_stats.f90->sourcefile~watch_log.f90 sourcefile~watch_types.f90 watch_types.f90 sourcefile~watch_feat_stats.f90->sourcefile~watch_types.f90 sourcefile~watch_feature_api.f90->sourcefile~watch_types.f90 sourcefile~watch_log.f90->sourcefile~watch_types.f90 sourcefile~watch_util.f90 watch_util.f90 sourcefile~watch_log.f90->sourcefile~watch_util.f90 Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Source File Source File This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a file to a file which it depends on. A file\nis dependent upon another if the latter must be compiled before the former\ncan be. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Files dependent on this one sourcefile~~watch_feat_stats.f90~~AfferentGraph sourcefile~watch_feat_stats.f90 watch_feat_stats.f90 sourcefile~watch_feature_factory.f90 watch_feature_factory.f90 sourcefile~watch_feature_factory.f90->sourcefile~watch_feat_stats.f90 sourcefile~watch_engine.f90 watch_engine.f90 sourcefile~watch_engine.f90->sourcefile~watch_feature_factory.f90 Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Source File Source File This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a file to a file which it depends on. A file\nis dependent upon another if the latter must be compiled before the former\ncan be. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code !> Example \"stats\" feature plugin. !! !! This feature demonstrates the plugin system by tracking the number of runs. !! It currently does not print or export statistics; it simply increments an !! internal counter after each run. !! !! The `exitstat` and `seconds` arguments are accepted to conform to the plugin !! interface. Dummy references are used to prevent unused-argument warnings. module watch_feat_stats use watch_feature_api , only : watch_feature_t use watch_types , only : watch_config_t use watch_log , only : log_event implicit none private public new_stats_feature !> A feature that counts how many command runs have completed. type , extends ( watch_feature_t ) :: stats_feature_t !> Completed command cycles, including failed or interrupted commands. integer :: runs = 0 contains procedure :: init => stats_init procedure :: on_after_run => stats_after end type stats_feature_t contains !> Allocate a new stats feature instance. subroutine new_stats_feature ( f ) class ( watch_feature_t ), allocatable , intent ( out ) :: f allocate ( stats_feature_t :: f ) end subroutine new_stats_feature !> Initialize the stats feature. !! !! Currently logs a single \"feature enabled\" message. subroutine stats_init ( self , cfg ) class ( stats_feature_t ), intent ( inout ) :: self type ( watch_config_t ), intent ( in ) :: cfg self % runs = 0 if ( cfg % w % verbosity >= 1 ) call log_event ( \"info\" , \"feature.enabled\" , \"Feature enabled: stats\" , verbosity = 1 ) end subroutine stats_init !> Increment the internal completed-command counter for every outcome; no statistics are emitted. subroutine stats_after ( self , exitstat , seconds ) class ( stats_feature_t ), intent ( inout ) :: self integer , intent ( in ) :: exitstat real , intent ( in ) :: seconds self % runs = self % runs + 1 if (. false .) then if ( exitstat /= 0 ) continue if ( seconds < 0.0 ) continue end if end subroutine stats_after end module watch_feat_stats","tags":"","url":"sourcefile/watch_feat_stats.f90.html"},{"title":"watch_filter.f90 – fpm-watch","text":"This file depends on sourcefile~~watch_filter.f90~~EfferentGraph sourcefile~watch_filter.f90 watch_filter.f90 sourcefile~watch_util.f90 watch_util.f90 sourcefile~watch_filter.f90->sourcefile~watch_util.f90 Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Source File Source File This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a file to a file which it depends on. A file\nis dependent upon another if the latter must be compiled before the former\ncan be. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Files dependent on this one sourcefile~~watch_filter.f90~~AfferentGraph sourcefile~watch_filter.f90 watch_filter.f90 sourcefile~watch_fpm_graph.f90 watch_fpm_graph.f90 sourcefile~watch_fpm_graph.f90->sourcefile~watch_filter.f90 sourcefile~watch_engine.f90 watch_engine.f90 sourcefile~watch_engine.f90->sourcefile~watch_fpm_graph.f90 Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Source File Source File This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a file to a file which it depends on. A file\nis dependent upon another if the latter must be compiled before the former\ncan be. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code !> Normalize paths and filter the computed watch list with fpm globs. !! !! Normalization changes backslashes to `/` and removes leading `./` segments; !! it does not canonicalize paths. Build-directory exclusion is applied by the !! graph collector. Include/ignore filters always retain watched `fpm.toml` files. module watch_filter use watch_util , only : starts_with use , intrinsic :: iso_fortran_env , only : int64 use fpm_strings , only : string_t , glob use fpm_filesystem , only : basename implicit none private public normalize_path , starts_with , filter_watch_files , is_ignored_path contains !> Right-trim a path, replace backslashes, and remove repeated leading `./`. !! !! Preserve case, interior separators, and `..` segments; no filesystem lookup !! or canonicalization is performed. pure function normalize_path ( path ) result ( p ) character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: path character ( len = :), allocatable :: p integer :: i p = trim ( path ) do i = 1 , len ( p ) if ( p ( i : i ) == char ( 92 )) p ( i : i ) = \"/\" end do do while ( len ( p ) >= 2 ) if ( p ( 1 : 2 ) == \"./\" ) then p = p ( 3 :) else exit end if end do end function normalize_path !> Compare right-trimmed suffixes; an empty suffix matches every string. pure logical function ends_with ( s , suffix ) result ( ok ) character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: s , suffix integer :: ns , n ns = len_trim ( suffix ) n = len_trim ( s ) if ( ns <= 0 ) then ok = . true . else if ( n < ns ) then ok = . false . else ok = ( s ( n - ns + 1 : n ) == suffix ( 1 : ns )) end if end function ends_with !> Test for a slash-delimited interior or trailing path fragment. pure logical function contains_path_fragment ( p , frag ) result ( ok ) character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: p , frag character ( len = :), allocatable :: needle ok = . false . if ( len_trim ( frag ) == 0 ) return needle = \"/\" // trim ( frag ) // \"/\" if ( index ( p , needle ) /= 0 ) then ok = . true . return end if if ( ends_with ( p , \"/\" // trim ( frag ))) then ok = . true . return end if end function contains_path_fragment !> Test normalized paths against a build directory or its slash-delimited fragments. !! !! Matches the directory itself, its descendants, and interior/trailing path !! fragments. An empty build directory matches nothing; dependency exceptions !! are applied by the graph collector. pure logical function is_ignored_path ( path , build_dir ) result ( ignored ) character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: path , build_dir character ( len = :), allocatable :: p , b p = normalize_path ( path ) b = normalize_path ( build_dir ) ignored = . false . if ( len_trim ( b ) == 0 ) return if ( p == b ) then ignored = . true . return end if if ( starts_with ( p , b // \"/\" )) then ignored = . true . return end if if ( contains_path_fragment ( p , b )) then ignored = . true . return end if end function is_ignored_path !> Filter normalized paths and optional aligned masks, preserving order. !! !! Empty/unallocated include lists admit all files; matching ignore globs !! remove included paths. A basename of `fpm.toml` bypasses both filters. !! An unallocated file list is left unchanged. If masks are supplied but !! unallocated, create zero masks before filtering them with the files. subroutine filter_watch_files ( files , include , ignore , masks ) type ( string_t ), allocatable , intent ( inout ) :: files (:) !! Normalized input paths, replaced with the retained subset in the same order. type ( string_t ), allocatable , intent ( in ) :: include (:), ignore (:) !! Include and ignore glob lists, respectively; empty/unallocated lists impose no restriction. integer ( int64 ), allocatable , intent ( inout ), optional :: masks (:) !! Aligned file masks; created as zeros if supplied but unallocated, then filtered with files. logical , allocatable :: keep (:) type ( string_t ), allocatable :: out (:) integer ( int64 ), allocatable :: outm (:) integer :: i , k , n , m if (. not . allocated ( files )) return n = size ( files ) allocate ( keep ( n )) do i = 1 , n keep ( i ) = should_keep ( files ( i )% s , include , ignore ) end do m = count ( keep ) allocate ( out ( m )) if ( present ( masks )) then if (. not . allocated ( masks )) then allocate ( masks ( n )) masks = 0_int64 end if allocate ( outm ( m )) end if k = 0 do i = 1 , n if (. not . keep ( i )) cycle k = k + 1 out ( k )% s = files ( i )% s if ( present ( masks )) outm ( k ) = masks ( i ) end do call move_alloc ( out , files ) if ( present ( masks )) call move_alloc ( outm , masks ) deallocate ( keep ) contains !> Retain manifests unconditionally; otherwise require inclusion and no ignore match. logical function should_keep ( path , include_pats , ignore_pats ) result ( ok ) character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: path type ( string_t ), allocatable , intent ( in ) :: include_pats (:), ignore_pats (:) character ( len = :), allocatable :: b b = basename ( trim ( path )) if ( b == \"fpm.toml\" ) then ok = . true . return end if ok = match_include ( path , include_pats ) . and . (. not . match_any ( path , ignore_pats )) end function should_keep !> Test fpm globs after normalizing each nonempty pattern; an absent list matches nothing. logical function match_any ( path , pats ) result ( ok ) character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: path type ( string_t ), allocatable , intent ( in ) :: pats (:) integer :: j character ( len = :), allocatable :: pat ok = . false . if (. not . allocated ( pats )) return do j = 1 , size ( pats ) if ( len_trim ( pats ( j )% s ) == 0 ) cycle pat = normalize_path ( trim ( pats ( j )% s )) if ( glob ( trim ( path ), pat )) then ok = . true . return end if end do end function match_any !> Admit every path for an empty/unallocated include list; otherwise require a glob match. logical function match_include ( path , pats ) result ( ok ) character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: path type ( string_t ), allocatable , intent ( in ) :: pats (:) if (. not . allocated ( pats )) then ok = . true . return end if if ( size ( pats ) == 0 ) then ok = . true . return end if ok = match_any ( path , pats ) end function match_include end subroutine filter_watch_files end module watch_filter","tags":"","url":"sourcefile/watch_filter.f90.html"},{"title":"watch_time.f90 – fpm-watch","text":"Files dependent on this one sourcefile~~watch_time.f90~~AfferentGraph sourcefile~watch_time.f90 watch_time.f90 sourcefile~watch_engine.f90 watch_engine.f90 sourcefile~watch_engine.f90->sourcefile~watch_time.f90 sourcefile~watch_restart.f90 watch_restart.f90 sourcefile~watch_restart.f90->sourcefile~watch_time.f90 Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Source File Source File This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a file to a file which it depends on. A file\nis dependent upon another if the latter must be compiled before the former\ncan be. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code !> Choose busy waiting or OS sleep for polling, debounce, and restart delays. !! !! `set_low_cpu` selects a process-wide backend: `system_clock` spin waiting !! by default, or the C sleep shim when enabled. Windows uses `Sleep` with !! millisecond rounding; POSIX uses `nanosleep`, retrying the remaining duration !! after EINTR. These waits do not poll the watcher's termination flag. module watch_time use , intrinsic :: iso_fortran_env , only : int64 use , intrinsic :: iso_c_binding , only : c_double implicit none private public sleep_seconds , set_low_cpu !> Global mode switch that selects OS sleep vs spin sleep. !! !! - `.false.` (default): `sleep_seconds` uses `sleep_spin` (busy wait). !! - `.true.`           : `sleep_seconds` uses `sleep_os` (OS-backed sleep). logical , save :: low_cpu_mode = . false . !> Binding to the C implementation of portable OS sleep. interface !> C OS-sleep backend; `sec` is passed by value in seconds, with nonpositive values ignored. subroutine fpm_watch_sleep_seconds ( sec ) bind ( C , name = \"fpm_watch_sleep_seconds\" ) import c_double implicit none real ( c_double ), value :: sec end subroutine fpm_watch_sleep_seconds end interface contains !> Set the process-wide waiting backend: true selects OS sleep, false selects busy waiting. subroutine set_low_cpu ( flag ) logical , intent ( in ) :: flag low_cpu_mode = flag end subroutine set_low_cpu !> Wait approximately `s` seconds using the currently selected backend. !! !! Nonpositive values return immediately. OS sleep yields to the scheduler; !! spin waiting consumes CPU. Neither backend checks watcher cancellation. subroutine sleep_seconds ( s ) real , intent ( in ) :: s if ( s <= 0.0 ) return if ( low_cpu_mode ) then call sleep_os ( s ) else call sleep_spin ( s ) end if end subroutine sleep_seconds !> Busy-wait for the truncated number of system-clock ticks in `s` seconds. !! !! Nonpositive/sub-tick durations return immediately. A nonpositive clock !! rate falls back to 1000 ticks/s. The loop does not yield or check cancellation. subroutine sleep_spin ( s ) real , intent ( in ) :: s integer ( int64 ) :: rate , t0 , t1 , ticks if ( s <= 0.0 ) return call system_clock ( count_rate = rate ) if ( rate <= 0_int64 ) rate = 1000_int64 ticks = int ( s * real ( rate ), int64 ) if ( ticks <= 0_int64 ) return call system_clock ( t0 ) do call system_clock ( t1 ) if ( t1 - t0 >= ticks ) exit end do end subroutine sleep_spin !> Convert seconds to C double and invoke the OS-sleep shim; nonpositive durations are ignored there. subroutine sleep_os ( s ) real , intent ( in ) :: s call fpm_watch_sleep_seconds ( real ( s , c_double )) end subroutine sleep_os end module watch_time","tags":"","url":"sourcefile/watch_time.f90.html"}]}